today i'm going to be speaking with  spencer headworth who's assistant  professor of sociology at purdue  university affiliated scholar of the  american bar foundation and also author  of the book policing welfare punitive  adversarialism  in public assistance uh it's great to  have you on i really appreciate your  time  thanks for having me it's good to be  here so um you know i think maybe to get  us into this conversation oftentimes in  popular culture  people will point to  the ronald reagan stories about welfare  queens as really raising the level of  suspicion around welfare programs and  and so-called free riders  how far back can we trace that suspicion  about people receiving benefits they  don't really deserve in this type of  idea  sure uh so it goes back about as far as  organized poverty relief programs go  back so you can look at english poor law  in the 15th century in the 1400s in  england and in those very early versions  of assistance programs there were  provisions for  you know inspectors basically to check  up on people and see if they were  drinking or gambling with the assistance  that they were getting  from the state and in the united states  again it tracks back to the kind of  earliest versions of public assistance  programs that were out there  and  in the 20th century when we get uh  food stamps to predecessor to snap  uh the  kind of earliest food stamp uh  provisions included checking on people  to see if they were violating rules um  and there's it's a long history that's  intertwined uh there really is is almost  no uh historical period where welfare  programs did not include some kind of  suspicion checking evaluation types of  mechanisms  that said it certainly does ramp up uh  in the reagan administration in the  united states as a politically and  popularly salient issue and a period  when we get several key legal changes  so if a um certain program  has requirements in order to qualify  it seems intuitive that you would have  some mechanism for policing that and  ensuring that the people that are  getting the benefits actually qualify  now in in some theoretical sense  if  one eliminated all of this uh  bureaucracy for policing  there's some cost to that bureaucracy in  theory it might actually  in the net cost less not to worry about  the so-called free riders and eliminate  the cost of the bureaucracy has that  been studied with any particular  programs  uh so  economists have you know looked into  this at least to some degree with uh  regard to like a ubi type of system  right a universal basic income  uh the idea of you know taking a  means-tested program like snap like uh  tanf that we have now and completely  eliminating  the  expenses associated with the policing  mechanisms it's really hard to tell  you can do some things some very you  know basic you know back of the napkin  how much are we recovering per  investigation type of math but that does  not take account for whatever deterrent  effect the existence of fraud  investigation has  the  kind of broader symbolic uh components  of the system that have an influence on  people's behavior um which there is some  influence but it's really hard to  determine how it's uh you know the kind  of counterfactual where we don't have  these programs we don't have data really  right  so  it's tough to say and for the book i  uh the primary data are interviews with  people who work in fraud investigation  investigators and data analysts and  managers of these units and so forth and  they  you know  perhaps unsurprisingly given it's their  job but they think that that deterrent  effect which is hard to measure  is is really substantial so it's hard to  say  in uh in in policing by law enforcement  we  have many examples of how  a law or a statute can just kind of be  written  agnostically on paper but can be applied  in discriminatory ways or or selectively  can you talk a little bit about when it  comes to policing of welfare programs  does the same thing apply and if so how  do we see kind of the discriminate  discriminative application of some of  these programs or of policing mechanisms  rather  so there are ways uh that these programs  uh the way some of the ways that these  programs function  do have some implications i think for  inequities in who's investigated who's  uh you know potentially charged with  welfare fraud who has these charges  substantiated against them um you know i  will note that all the or nearly all of  the people that i spoke to were adamant  that they do not discriminate on the  basis of race they um you know have no  feelings about that one way or the other  uh you know one of the basic facts here  is that they mostly investigate women  because women are disproportionately  participants in the program right so  there's that uh there's that difference  i guess the question there would be are  they investigated at a greater rate than  their level of participation  uh they they don't keep statistics on  the characteristics of the people that  they investigate or at least they told  me that they don't so i can't say that  for sure i can say that  for example being  a person with children as opposed to a  person without children  has at least some effect on increasing  your likelihood of becoming the subject  of a serious investigation uh so  the  uh one of the basic mechanisms here is  the they're trying to figure out which  cases are most promising so they have  more potential investigations than they  can pursue so they choose which ones to  go after right one of the main things  that they're looking for  is a higher dollar value so if you're  the head of a household with children  you're probably getting a higher dollar  value in benefits than if you're not so  that's an example of a just kind of a  mathematical risk factor another  thing that's going on here is  the cases that are most promising to  investigate are cases with more  evidentiary availability right so if you  can get more information about a person  you're more likely to spend more time on  that investigation to make it a priority  because you can get more access to  evidence that you can use to  substantiate your charges so that means  if you are you know quote-unquote on  paper uh in various ways already with  government agencies whether that be  criminal justice agencies agencies of  the welfare state whatever it is if you  have kids in school  all these are potential sources of  information  uh that welfare fraud investigators can  draw upon to figure out how much money  you make who lives in your house these  kinds of things  so being more  recorded by the state uh has this sort  of implication of also making you  potentially more interesting as someone  to investigate because this evidence is  out there in ways it might not be for  someone who's less recorded with the  state when we talk about fraud um  people  oftentimes the application processes for  these programs are not particularly  simple  um and there's it seems to me at least  an intent a big difference between fraud  and errors made during the application  process can you talk a little bit about  whether mistakes and errors or  information that has since changed are  are considered fraud under this general  umbrella  um so i mean first i would flag though  the person whose research is probably  the best on this is karen gustafson who  wrote a book called cheating welfare  that's one of the inspirations for this  book that came out a few years ago  with nyu press and you know she focuses  on clients  and so she she delves pretty deeply into  this question of  intent and the construction of intent  and when is a mistake just a mistake  one thing that i can say for sure from  my  uh evidence and comparing it with  previous work like karen's that looks at  the the client side  is that the investigators are much more  likely to see things as intentional and  present them as intentional than our  clients right  and  that is in part due to the  structural factors pertaining here so  they are the individual investigators  and the broader units of which they are  parts are incentivized to  get intentional  program violation charges intentional  fraud charges those are the sort of most  rewarding for them professionally they  also garner  financial rewards for the state from the  federal government when you can uh  substantiate these types of charges so  uh there are things that i think are  borderline um that get constructed as  intentional and i mean the the broad  the you know the sort of baseline  understanding here is that if you  provide a piece of information to the  agency  uh and sign your application form saying  that everything that you've sworn to  here is accurate and up to date and then  later they find evidence whether it's  from the department of labor the  department of motor vehicles or a  privately maintained database of some  kind wherever they get it  if they find that evidence that  contradicts whatever you put on your  sign statement  that is generally considered probative  proof of  intentional fraud  and what is the process for adjudicating  something like that  so in 1976 uh the u.s supreme court  ruled in uh goldberg versus kelly that  welfare benefits are a form of property  uh and that therefore  uh participants in the program are  entitled to  some version of due process before  benefits can be terminated  um so the way that that works when  you're accused of an intentional program  violation is  in a setting that's called an  administrative hearing  uh administrative qualification hearing  perhaps uh that is overseen by an  administrative hearing officer that is  the equivalent of a judge  and that's where the client gets an  opportunity to come in and  potentially argue against uh whatever  allegations are being made present their  own evidence if they have evidence that  contradicts the evidence  that the welfare fraud investigator  gathered  and then the administrative hearing  officer makes a determination of whether  the state has met their  burden of proof in establishing you know  clear and convincing evidence of an  intentional program violation so that's  one of the things that welfare fraud  investigators have to learn how to  navigate and negotiate is  how to work in these settings getting to  know the particular hearing officers and  what kind of standards they have and  it's you know one sort of advantage that  a  fraud investigator has over a client is  they're more familiar with the setting  right they potentially know the hearing  officer they've been there before they  know  you know ms jones likes this kind of  evidence mr smith doesn't like this kind  of evidence right you know they're more  inclined to do this or that um so that's  the that's the setting where these  things are adjudicated and  one of the things that fraud  investigators learned is how to navigate  and negotiate in those spaces  one of the um  things i wanted to talk a little bit  about is  just other systems in other countries  that may overall be more efficient and  one could argue you know part of it  depends on your perspective but one  could argue that there's lots of  inefficiencies in in these systems one  could argue that the means testing  itself is an inefficiency one could  argue that the methods for policing are  inefficient  there's a lot of different approaches to  that but  in terms of benefits and cost efficiency  overall  what should we be aspiring to what sort  of examples in other countries are  particularly well run  uh so i'm not an expert on international  uh welfare states uh but i will say that  there is i think a very strong case to  be made for a universal basic income  type of system where you remove  all of this administrative overhead all  of this bureaucratic red tape and  create a much smoother flowing system  without all of these hurdles and  barriers and all the associated costs  and the you know expenses uh and so  people who are  uh experts in this area at least to the  extent that i've read them you know uh  generally  uh associate a system like ours that  focuses on means testing uh with the  almost inherent or uh  inescapable circumstance of  rule enforcement being a big part of it  right and this  process of determining who's in and  who's out being  a costly endeavor so in a system where  you can eliminate those  administrative expenses you're saving  that money so i think that there's a  definitely a good case to be made for  more of a uh  universal system so i mean something  like we have now in the united states  but just based on age right for social  security benefits and medicare benefits  in such a system  when both when it comes to ubi and  so-called free college  there's the criticism that you're also  giving stuff to the very very rich now  they're very very rich statistically are  a small percentage of of the economy by  by sort of definition is that something  we should worry about or not  well i mean i won't tell people what to  worry about or what to not worry about  but there  i mean there is an inherent logic to a  means-tested system right it makes a lot  of sense i think in an abstract world or  just you know on paper that people in  need should be the people that receive  things but it just gets very complicated  when you come down to the process okay  what is need right how are we gonna  calculate that that's contentious in and  of itself and then yeah these these  administrative costs i think  um they're often not visible publicly  right or not really prominent in the  popular imagination and again there is  this inherent logic to  targeting  aid to needy people  but where the rubber hits the road it's  a lot more complicated than just  see who's needy give it to them don't  give it to someone who's not yeah yeah  that's for sure that's for sure  the book is policing welfare punitive  adversarialism and public assistance  we've been speaking with the book's  author spencer headworth who's also  assistant professor of sociology at  purdue university  so great having you on today and really  appreciate your time  thanks david it was a pleasure

